                <pb facs="0902" n="895" sameAs="frus:frus1937v03/pg895"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">693.002/413: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Counselor of Embassy in China</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Lockhart</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peiping</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1937-12-03T18:00:00+08:00">December 3, 1937—6
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received December 3—7 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>801. Your 384, November 30, 1 p.m. Information contained in your above-mentioned
                    telegram was repeated to Tientsin which has now replied as follows.</p>
                <quote rend="blockquote">
                    <p>“December 3, 10 a.m. Your December 1, 6 p.m.</p>
                    <p>Commissioner of Customs states most emphatically that duties, both import and
                        export collected in accordance with the established Chinese customs tariff
                        and the question of reduction of duties on Japanese goods has not been
                        discussed but that all Japanese military supplies enter duty free and
                        without examination by customs. This office is unable to obtain any
                        information contrary to this statement.</p>
                    <p>Repeated to Consul General, Hong Kong in reply to his inquiry of December 1,
                        11 a.m.”</p>
                </quote>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Lockhart</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
